course: dictionary
lesson: 880
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Bingo is a game. Players have cards covered in numbers, and a caller reads numbers out. Each player crosses off the numbers they hear, and the first player to finish a line or the whole card wins.
explain: Bingo as a game is a noun, and we do not add an s to it. We say «play bingo», not «play bingos». You will also see «bingo hall» and «bingo card».
explain: Bingo can also be a shout. When you suddenly know the answer, you can say «Bingo!», which means «Yes! I've found it» or «Exactly right». Context tells you which meaning is used: is the word talking about a game, or is someone shouting with joy?
explain: Look at «Picture bingo in Liverpool: the teacher calls "soixante-seize" and Ethan must wait for the whole number before he marks 76, not 60 or 16.» The clues say the game is about matching the exact number, so you must wait for all of it.
fact: Bingo is a game where players cross off numbers on their cards as they are called out.
fact: The first player to cross off a full line or card wins, and they shout «Bingo!»
fact: Bingo is played at fairs, holiday camps, care homes, church halls and classrooms.
fact: In maths lessons, bingo practises number facts and times tables.
fact: Informal «Bingo!» means «Yes! I've found it» or «Exactly right».
exercise: In bingo, what do players cross off on their cards? | The caller's microphone | Pictures of animals | The names of the other players | *The numbers that are called out
exercise: In «the teacher calls "soixante-seize"», what number is that? | 60 | 16 | *76 | 97
exercise: When someone shouts «Bingo!» after solving a sum, what does it mean? | *Yes, I've found it, or exactly right | I want to stop playing now | It is time for lunch | The caller is wrong
test: Which of these describes the game meaning of bingo? | *A game with numbered cards, where the first to cross off a full card wins | A kind of dog that waits at the door | To shout loudly across a hall | Yes, I've found it!
test: Which sentence uses bingo correctly as a noun for the game? | We played bingoes all afternoon | We bingoed all afternoon | We played bingos all afternoon | *We played bingo all afternoon
exam: In «Mrs Lee bought the park kids ice lollies with her bingo money», what does bingo money mean? | Money for a holiday camp | *Money from playing the bingo game | Money given to a dog | Money found on the floor
